w some areas to remain almost unresolved while focal areas become sharply understandable. Movement is a major opportunity. Use repeated positions, ghosted limbs, abandoned wing locations, gestural arcs and overlapping anatomy to show where a body, animal or object has just been and where it is moving. Do not use generic cartoon speed lines. Sketch is a visual language, not a subject category. The subject can be anything. Portraits. Human figures. Hands. Animals. Birds. Horses. Dragons. Skulls. Skeletons. Demons. Angels. Dark fantasy. Gothic architecture. Mythological creatures. Horror. Botanicals. Machines. Motorcycles. Cars. Fashion. Architecture. Anatomy. Surrealism. Impossible creatures. Abstract drawing. Do not keep the imagery polite. Some tattoos should be beautiful. Some should be intimate. Some should be elegant. Some should be dark. Some should be aggressive. Some should be disturbing. Some should feel enormous and mythological. Some should make the viewer wonder what the hell they are looking at before the subject suddenly resolves. Push artistic ambition rather than novelty. Do not turn dark artwork into cheap gore or juvenile shock imagery. Darkness should come through composition, anatomy, mythology, mortality, psychological tension, scale, atmosphere and drawing energy. Black and natural skin should dominate much of the collection. Color may be used as part of the drawing process. Pale blue can represent preliminary construction. Red or burgundy can represent correction, alternative positioning or an additional drawing layer. Black can establish the final committed drawing. These relationships are possibilities rather than mandatory rules. Color must not become random decoration. Do not turn Sketch into Watercolour. Do not use full realistic color unless the concept specifically requires a hybrid. Hatching and cross-hatching should describe form and directional shadow. Scribble and charcoal-like marks should carry weight, movement or atmosphere rather 