 Construction lines should relate logically to the subject. Human heads may use cranial masses, centerlines and eye axes. Hands may use joint circles and finger direction. Animals may expose skeletal or muscular construction. Machines and architecture may retain perspective axes and ellipses. Dragons and invented creatures should be constructed convincingly enough that their imaginary anatomy feels studied. Design every composition specifically for the stated body placement. THE BODY IS THE PAGE, BUT THE PAGE IS NOT FLAT. Use forearms for elongated drawings, perspective, smaller portraits and technical studies. Use upper arms and shoulders for portraits, creatures and compositions that benefit from wrapping construction. Use thighs for large figures, dark fantasy, horses, dragons and complicated drawing studies. Use calves for strong vertical compositions. Use ribs for gesture and elongated human figures. Use backs for rare major pieces where wings, anatomy, architecture or large drawing systems need space. Let construction and movement respond to body curvature rather than appearing printed onto the skin. Do not make every design huge. Most should remain wearable small and medium pieces. Use large scale where the artistic idea genuinely benefits from it. Reserve full backs, chest compositions and major limb projects for exceptional concepts. Avoid repetitive wolves, lions, roses and womens faces unless the treatment is genuinely exceptional. Avoid random sketch marks. Avoid fake handwritten notes. Avoid meaningless arrows. Avoid decorative geometry unrelated to construction. Avoid generic splatter. Avoid clean finished tattoos with superficial scribbles around them. Avoid making every drawing equally chaotic. Contrast beautifully resolved areas against unresolved areas. THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A BAD SKETCH AND A GREAT SKETCH TATTOO IS INTENTION. A BAD SKETCH LOOKS UNFINISHED BECAUSE IT WAS NOT FINISHED. A GREAT SKETCH TATTOO LOOKS UNFINISHED BECAUSE THAT IS THE FINISHED ART'
